---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import FAQSection from "../components/FAQSection.astro";
---

<Layout title="How to Budget for a Disney Vacation">
  <style>
    .container {
      max-width: 960px;
      margin: 0 auto;
      padding: 72px 20px;
    }

    .hero {
      text-align: center;
      max-width: 820px;
      margin: 0 auto 72px;
    }

    .eyebrow {
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 3px;
      font-size: 0.8rem;
      color: var(--accent);
      font-weight: 800;
      margin-bottom: 18px;
    }

    h1 {
      font-size: clamp(2.6rem, 7vw, 4.7rem);
      line-height: 1;
      margin: 0 0 24px;
    }

    .subtitle {
      color: var(--muted);
      font-size: 1.2rem;
      line-height: 1.7;
      margin-bottom: 32px;
    }

    .cta-link {
      display: inline-block;
      background: var(--button);
      color: white;
      text-decoration: none;
      font-weight: 800;
      padding: 16px 24px;
      border-radius: 14px;
    }

    .content {
      max-width: 760px;
      margin: 0 auto;
    }

    section {
      margin: 72px 0 0;
    }

    h2 {
      font-size: 2rem;
      margin-bottom: 18px;
    }

    p {
      color: var(--muted);
      line-height: 1.8;
      margin-bottom: 18px;
    }

    ul {
      color: var(--muted);
      line-height: 1.8;
      padding-left: 22px;
    }

    li {
      margin-bottom: 12px;
    }

    .callout {
      background: var(--card);
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 24px;
      padding: 28px;
      margin-top: 32px;
    }

    .link-grid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr));
      gap: 18px;
      margin-top: 26px;
    }

    .link-card {
      display: block;
      background: var(--card);
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 20px;
      padding: 22px;
      text-decoration: none;
      color: inherit;
    }

    .link-card strong {
      display: block;
      margin-bottom: 8px;
    }

    .link-card span {
      color: var(--muted);
      line-height: 1.5;
    }
  </style>

  <main class="container">
    <div class="hero">
      <div class="eyebrow">Disney Vacation Guide</div>

      <h1>How to Budget for a Disney Vacation</h1>

      <p class="subtitle">
        A Disney trip can be memorable, expensive, and easy to underestimate.
        This guide breaks down the real costs, common budget traps, and smarter ways
        to decide whether the trip fits your financial life.
      </p>

      <a class="cta-link" href="/should-i-spend-8000-on-a-disney-vacation">
        Use the Disney Vacation Calculator
      </a>
    </div>

    <div class="content">
      <section>
        <h2>What a Disney Vacation Actually Costs</h2>

        <p>
          A Disney vacation budget should include more than hotel and park tickets.
          The real cost often includes airfare or gas, airport transfers, rental cars,
          parking, meals, snacks, souvenirs, stroller rentals, travel insurance,
          Lightning Lane-style upgrades, and emergency spending.
        </p>

        <p>
          A family can easily underestimate the trip by focusing on the big-ticket items
          and ignoring daily costs inside the parks. Food, drinks, convenience purchases,
          and small add-ons can add hundreds or thousands of dollars by the end of the trip.
        </p>

        <div class="callout">
          <p>
            A safer Disney budget includes the full trip cost before booking, plus a cushion
            for weather, fatigue, transportation changes, and in-park convenience spending.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Biggest Disney Budget Mistakes</h2>

        <ul>
          <li>Budgeting for park tickets and hotel, but forgetting food and snacks.</li>
          <li>Assuming the advertised price reflects the full trip cost.</li>
          <li>Ignoring airfare, checked bags, rental cars, or airport transfers.</li>
          <li>Adding premium park upgrades without planning for them upfront.</li>
          <li>Using credit cards without a clear payoff plan.</li>
          <li>Leaving no emergency cushion after the trip.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          The biggest mistake is treating Disney like a normal vacation. Disney has more
          emotional pressure, more upsell opportunities, and more in-the-moment spending
          decisions than many trips.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Disney Hotel Strategy</h2>

        <p>
          Hotel choice can change the entire budget. Staying on property may offer convenience,
          shorter travel time, and a more immersive experience, but it can also raise the total
          cost substantially.
        </p>

        <p>
          Off-property hotels can save money, especially for families willing to handle more
          transportation planning. The tradeoff is time, convenience, parking, shuttles, and
          how tired everyone feels after long park days.
        </p>

        <p>
          The best hotel choice is not always the cheapest one. A slightly more convenient hotel
          can be worth it if it prevents rideshare costs, parking stress, long commutes, or
          mid-trip burnout.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Park Ticket Strategy</h2>

        <p>
          Park tickets are one of the largest Disney expenses. More park days usually mean a
          higher total trip cost, but fewer park days can create pressure to rush through everything.
        </p>

        <p>
          Park hopper tickets can add flexibility, but they are not always necessary. Families
          trying to control costs may prefer one park per day, especially if they are visiting
          with younger children or first-time Disney travelers.
        </p>

        <p>
          Before buying tickets, decide how many full park days your family can realistically
          handle. Heat, walking, lines, and overstimulation can make rest time more valuable
          than one extra park day.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Lightning Lane, Genie+, and Paid Convenience</h2>

        <p>
          Paid line-skipping and premium access can improve the trip, but they should be treated
          as planned budget items rather than surprise upgrades.
        </p>

        <p>
          These tools may be worth it for families with limited park days, young children, older
          relatives, or a strong desire to experience major rides without spending the day in lines.
          They are less necessary if the trip is longer, the itinerary is flexible, or the budget
          is already tight.
        </p>

        <p>
          The key is deciding before the trip. In the park, tired families are more likely to buy
          convenience under pressure.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Food and Souvenir Budgeting</h2>

        <p>
          Food and souvenirs are where Disney budgets often break. Quick-service meals, snacks,
          drinks, character dining, themed treats, and impulse purchases can add up quickly.
        </p>

        <p>
          A practical approach is to set a daily food budget and a separate souvenir budget before
          the trip. Families with children may also want to give each child a set souvenir amount
          so every purchase does not become a new negotiation.
        </p>

        <p>
          Character meals and table-service restaurants can be memorable, but they should be chosen
          intentionally. A few planned splurges are easier to manage than constant unplanned spending.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Cheapest Times to Go</h2>

        <p>
          Disney prices can vary by season, demand, hotel availability, school calendars, and holidays.
          Cheaper windows often come with tradeoffs, such as heat, rain, shorter hours, or less convenient
          school schedules.
        </p>

        <p>
          Families trying to lower the total cost should compare travel dates before locking in flights
          or hotel rooms. Moving the trip by a week or two can sometimes change airfare, hotel rates,
          and crowd levels.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>When a Disney Trip Is Financially Worth It</h2>

        <p>
          A Disney trip is easier to justify when it is paid from savings, does not drain the emergency
          fund, and does not create high-interest debt after the vacation.
        </p>

        <p>
          It may also be worth it when the timing is meaningful: kids are at the right age, the trip
          marks a milestone, or the family has saved specifically for the experience.
        </p>

        <p>
          The healthiest version of the trip is the one that creates memories without creating financial
          stress after everyone gets home.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>When You Should Wait</h2>

        <p>
          Waiting may be the better choice if the trip would drain savings, require credit card debt,
          delay debt payoff, or make monthly bills harder to manage.
        </p>

        <p>
          Waiting does not mean giving up. A later trip may allow better flights, better hotel choices,
          more park flexibility, and less stress during the vacation itself.
        </p>
      </section>

      <FAQSection
        title="Disney Vacation Budget FAQ"
        faqs={[
          {
            question: "How much should a Disney trip cost?",
            answer:
              "A Disney trip can cost a few thousand dollars or well over $10,000 depending on family size, hotel choice, park days, airfare, meals, souvenirs, and paid upgrades. The safest budget includes the full trip cost plus a cushion.",
          },
          {
            question: "Is Disney worth the money?",
            answer:
              "Disney can be worth the money if the trip fits your savings, does not create debt pressure, and matters to your family. It becomes harder to justify when the cost creates stress after the trip.",
          },
          {
            question: "How much spending money should I bring to Disney?",
            answer:
              "Spending money depends on meals, snacks, souvenirs, stroller rentals, tips, and extras. Families should set a daily budget before the trip and include a separate cushion for unexpected costs.",
          },
          {
            question: "Is staying on property worth it?",
            answer:
              "Staying on property can be worth it for convenience, transportation, and the full Disney experience. Off-property lodging may be better when the priority is lowering the total trip cost.",
          },
          {
            question: "Should I use a credit card for a Disney vacation?",
            answer:
              "Using a credit card for points or protections can be reasonable if the balance is paid off immediately. Carrying a balance can make the trip much more expensive because of interest charges.",
          },
        ]}
      />

      <section>
        <h2>Plan the Trip Before You Book</h2>

        <p>
          Before booking, run the numbers against your actual income, savings, debt, and emergency fund.
          A Disney trip should feel exciting before you leave and financially manageable after you return.
        </p>

        <div class="link-grid">
          <a class="link-card" href="/should-i-spend-8000-on-a-disney-vacation">
            <strong>Disney Vacation Calculator</strong>
            <span>Estimate whether an $8,000 Disney trip fits your finances.</span>
          </a>

          <a class="link-card" href="/should-i-spend-5000-on-a-vacation">
            <strong>Vacation Calculator</strong>
            <span>Check a general travel budget against savings and debt.</span>
          </a>

          <a class="link-card" href="/travel-calculators">
            <strong>Travel Calculators</strong>
            <span>Browse Disney, Europe, cruise, honeymoon, and family vacation tools.</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>